378 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 760 0 R
/E (In this Arduino program, a push button is connected to pin 2 \(pushSwitch\) and an RGB LED is connected to pins 10 \(rgbRed\), 12 \(rgbGreen\), and 13 \(rgbBlue\).)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
379 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 761 0 R
/E (The setup\(\) function runs once when the Arduino starts. It sets the push button as INPUT_PULLUP and the RGB LED pins as OUTPUT using pinMode\(\).)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
380 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 762 0 R
/E (The loop\(\) function runs continuously. It reads the push button state using digitalRead\(\) and stores it in switchState.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
381 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 763 0 R
/E (If the button is pressed \(LOW signal\), the RGB LED changes colors in sequence. First, the red LED turns ON for 500 milliseconds, then the green LED turns ON for 500 milliseconds, and finally the blue LED turns ON for 500 milliseconds.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
382 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 764 0 R
/E (If the button is not pressed \(HIGH signal\), all three RGB LED colors remain OFF.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
383 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 765 0 R
/E (So overall, pressing the push button makes the RGB LED cycle through red, green, and blue colors, while releasing the button turns the RGB LED OFF.)
/Pg 21 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
