481 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 786 0 R
/E (In an Arduino program, first we define the pins or variables. This means we give a name to a pin number so it is easy to use in the code. For example, if we write int ledPin = 9; or int buzzerPin = 8;, it means we are using those pin numbers and giving them simple names.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [21]
>>
endobj
482 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 787 0 R
/E (Next comes the setup\(\) function. This part runs only one time when the Arduino is turned on. It is used to prepare the pins and start things like the Serial Monitor. Inside setup, we use pinMode\(\) to tell the Arduino what each pin will do. If we write pinMode\(ledPin, OUTPUT\);, it means the pin will send signals \(like turning an LED on or off\), and if we use INPUT, the pin will read signals \(like from a button\).)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [22]
>>
endobj
483 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 788 0 R
/E (After that comes the loop\(\) function. This part runs again and again forever. Arduino keeps repeating whatever is written inside loop, so it can continuously control or check the circuit.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
484 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 789 0 R
/E (Inside the loop, we can use analogWrite\(\) to control the brightness of an LED or the speed of a motor using PWM. The value ranges from 0 \(off\) to 255 \(full brightness\). We can also use tone\(\) to generate sound from a buzzer by giving a frequency, and noTone\(\) to stop the sound.)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
485 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 103 0 R
/E (So overall, the program first sets everything in setup\(\), then keeps running the loop continuously, controlling devices like LEDs and buzzers using functions such as pinMode\(\), analogWrite\(\), tone\(\), and noTone\(\).)
/Pg 25 0 R
/K [25]
>>
endobj
